# Which service holds accounts' keys, and under what names.
#
# accounts holds two keys: the Ed25519 signing key behind every access token and
# the published JWKS, and the envelope key that seals stored credentials —
# source tokens, MFA seeds, WebAuthn credentials, webhook secrets — together
# with the keyed hash behind every API key.
#
# Which service holds them is a DEPLOYMENT decision and is selected here, never
# inferred. The two shapes have opposite custody models, so an unstated backend
# is refused by name rather than defaulted into either:
#
#   vault  an in-cluster HashiCorp Vault: KV v2 for the signing key, Transit for
#          the envelope key and the keyed hash, reached with the AppRole
#          credential the `vault` groups carry. A stateful secrets store the cell
#          runs, unseals, backs up and credentials.
#   kms    the cell's cloud key-management service: keys that cannot be
#          exported, reached with the workload's own cloud identity. No secrets
#          store to run and no stored credential to rotate — the keys below are
#          NAMES, and nothing here is a credential or a file.
#
#   KEY_SERVICE_BACKEND           seals stored credentials and computes the keyed
#                                 hash. REQUIRED outside the local environment.
#   KEY_SERVICE_PREVIOUS_BACKEND  the backend a cutover is migrating AWAY from.
#                                 Accepted on READ only — nothing new is sealed
#                                 under it — and withdrawn once no stored value
#                                 references it. Naming the same backend twice is
#                                 refused: the re-seal sweep would be a no-op
#                                 while reading as progress.
#   KEY_SERVICE_SIGNING_BACKEND   holds the access-token signing key. Defaults to
#                                 KEY_SERVICE_BACKEND.
#
# The two families are selected separately because they move separately: an
# envelope key is migrated by re-sealing, which is reversible and invisible to
# users, while replacing the signing key either re-logs everyone in or needs both
# keys published across the cutover.
#
# The signing key has THREE possible homes, and the difference is a security
# property an operator chooses knowingly rather than one that arrives by default:
#
#   vault        the keypair lives in Vault KV v2 and accounts reads it at boot.
#   kms-wrapped  the key is a ciphertext in KEY_SERVICE_SIGNING_KEY_WRAPPED below
#                that only the cell's Cloud KMS key can turn back into a key.
#                NO SECRETS STORE: nothing to run, unseal, back up or credential,
#                and the stored value is inert without that key and the workload
#                identity that reaches it. The unwrapped key does live in process
#                memory, so this is non-exportable AT REST, not in use.
#   kms          the key never leaves Cloud KMS and every signature is a request.
#                REFUSED BY NAME today: three consumers are HANDED the signing key
#                rather than being given a signer to call — the Work Context
#                authority (core's workcontext.Authority, which accepts an
#                injected crypto.Signer that this host does not wire yet), the
#                delegation minter (github.com/codefly-dev/core
#                policy.MintEd25519) and the OAuth state signer's seed — so each
#                has to be given a signer first.
#
# A hosted cell that wants no Vault at all runs KEY_SERVICE_BACKEND=kms with
# KEY_SERVICE_SIGNING_BACKEND=kms-wrapped today, and moves to `kms` once those
# three take an injected signer.
KEY_SERVICE_BACKEND=vault
KEY_SERVICE_PREVIOUS_BACKEND=
KEY_SERVICE_SIGNING_BACKEND=

# The cloud keys, by resource name, when a backend above is `kms`. Each is read
# as a value; there is no credential and no file anywhere in this binding —
# the workload authenticates as itself (on GCP the metadata server answers for
# the service account bound to the pod).
#
# Each is named in the shape its own rotation semantics demand, and the asymmetry
# matters because getting it wrong is silent:
#
#   KEY_SERVICE_KMS_ENVELOPE_KEY  a cryptoKey, purpose ENCRYPT_DECRYPT, algorithm
#                                 GOOGLE_SYMMETRIC_ENCRYPTION. Encryption uses
#                                 the key's primary version and each ciphertext
#                                 records the version AND the key that sealed it,
#                                 so rotation never makes an existing ciphertext
#                                 unreadable, and a value sealed by a DIFFERENT
#                                 key is reported as a configuration error rather
#                                 than as a credential needing re-entry.
#                                 projects/<project>/locations/<location>/keyRings/<ring>/cryptoKeys/<key>
#   KEY_SERVICE_KMS_MAC_KEY       a cryptoKeyVERSION, algorithm HMAC_SHA256. The
#                                 keyed hash is what a presented API key is
#                                 looked up BY, so it must stay byte-identical
#                                 for the life of the key: moving to another
#                                 version silently stops every API key already
#                                 stored from matching. Pinning the version here
#                                 makes that a deliberate edit with a stated
#                                 consequence instead of a side effect of
#                                 rotating a key.
#                                 …/cryptoKeys/<key>/cryptoKeyVersions/<n>
#
# accounts validates both at boot — purpose, algorithm, version state, and a
# seal/open round trip — so a misnamed, wrongly-typed or unreachable key refuses
# to start while the deployment can still be fixed, rather than surfacing on the
# first MFA enrolment or API-key lookup.
KEY_SERVICE_KMS_ENVELOPE_KEY=
KEY_SERVICE_KMS_MAC_KEY=

# The `kms-wrapped` signing backend. A key of its own rather than the envelope
# key: destroying an envelope-key version would otherwise take away this host's
# ability to boot, and the two rotate on unrelated schedules.
#
#   KEY_SERVICE_KMS_SIGNING_WRAP_KEY  a cryptoKey, purpose ENCRYPT_DECRYPT,
#                                     algorithm GOOGLE_SYMMETRIC_ENCRYPTION
#   KEY_SERVICE_SIGNING_KEY_WRAPPED   the wrapped key, as a VALUE: the base64
#                                     Ed25519 seed sealed by the key above under
#                                     the associated data `signing-key-wrap`, in
#                                     the stored envelope framing
#                                     (cfs1:gcp-kms:<version>:<key>:<ciphertext>)
#
# The wrapped value is a ciphertext, not a credential: it is useless without the
# Cloud KMS key and the workload identity that reaches it, which is why it rides
# in this group as a value rather than as a secret or a mounted file.
#
# It must be the SAME value for every replica and across restarts — it is the
# host's identity, and the `kid` the gateway pinned moves if it changes. Producing
# it is the cell's provisioning, from the cell's durable seed, exactly as seeding
# Vault is on the `vault` backend: this module names no command of its own.
# accounts refuses a value wrapped by a different key, of the wrong size, or not
# in the envelope framing, each by name.
KEY_SERVICE_KMS_SIGNING_WRAP_KEY=
KEY_SERVICE_SIGNING_KEY_WRAPPED=

# Optional provider-owned managed installation. Supplied by the deployment
# owner; mutually exclusive with the legacy KMS key names above. The existing
# kms and kms-wrapped selections retain their custody and signing semantics.
KEY_SERVICE_MANAGED_BINDING=
